text:
Casey: Heads-up: onboarding sample is confusing new devs — first few lines at INFO without any request-id, then later DEBUG logs show request-id. Repro is pretty simple: start the sample, curl /health twice. Thoughts?
Devon: Which language/sample? Could be an init ordering or async exporter race.
Casey: Node + Rust versions in the repo. Node flow: top-level require('redwood-sdk') -> configure logging -> spin up OTLP exporter async -> listen. Rust flow spawns a background telemetry thread after logger init. Env: LOG_LEVEL=info.
Priya: Classic symptom: if the request-id injection or logger bootstrap happens after a component emits logs (or while an async OTLP exporter is registering), early logs won't have the id and the effective level can appear to flip.
Casey: I dug a bit — in the Node sample the logger module defers grabbing the configured level until the OTEL hook finishes registration which explains why we see INFO then DEBUG. In Rust the tracing subscriber is created lazily when the first instrumented span is started.
Devon: Short, safe fixes for samples: 1) make logger bootstrap synchronous at process entry (SDK.initLogger/init_logger/InitLogger depending on language); 2) add a tiny fallback request-id assigner that runs before route handlers or any background telemetry threads; 3) bump sample deps to an SDK that contains the exporter-level guard.
Casey: Can you paste minimal cross-language snippets we can drop into samples?
Devon: Node (top of index.js):
```js
// require this before imports that may log
const { initLogger } = require('redwood-sdk');
initLogger({ level: process.env.LOG_LEVEL || 'info', allowLevelFlip: false });
// tiny req-id fallback
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use((req,res,next)=>{ req.rid = req.headers['x-request-id'] || Date.now().toString(36); process.env._RID = req.rid; next(); });
module.exports = app;
```
Devon: Rust (main.rs) minimal pattern:
```rust
// top-level before threads spawn
redwood_sdk::logger::init_logger(redwood_sdk::logger::Config::from_env());
// request-id middleware for warp/axum
```
(then show small middleware that assigns a header-based id).
Priya: Also found PII leakage in DEBUG when headers printed — the sample used a handcrafted regex that missed plus signs, hyphens, and multi-part domains. Rather than reinventing it, call the SDK redaction helper where available. If you must use a pattern, use: `(?:[A-Za-z0-9._%+\-]+)@(?:[A-Za-z0-9.\-]+)\.[A-Za-z]{2,}` and test with `alice+ci@example.co.uk` and `bob.smith-qa@sub.domain.io`.
Casey: Any SDK version recommendations?
Devon: We added a guard in 1.4.x that prevents async OTEL exporter registration from toggling the effective logger level during startup — recommend bumping samples to >=1.4.1. Also the SDK exposes redact_headers and redact_env options so samples can call a single helper instead of ad-hoc patterns.
Priya: For the Rust sample I swapped the local regex for redwood_sdk::telemetry::redact::redact_headers(&mut headers) and added unit tests for header variants. For Node I used `redwood-sdk.redactHeaders(headers, {patterns: [...]})` in the sample helper.
Casey: We should also add a tiny CI smoke check: start the sample with LOG_LEVEL=info, hit /health once, capture stdout for a short timeframe, and assert that no DEBUG-level line appears before the first line that includes `request-id=`. Keep the window short to reduce flakiness.
Devon: Agreed. Also mirror the changes across Node, Rust, and Python samples so onboarding docs are consistent. We previously fixed Node only and then had confusion for other languages.
ops-bot: Created issue devex/samples#6012: "onboard: synchronous logger bootstrap + request-id fallback + use SDK redactor + add startup smoke test; bump to SDK >=1.4.1 (node/rust/python)"
Casey: I'll open a PR with: top-of-process init snippets, replace regex with SDK calls, add two tiny tests (one unit for redaction, one CI smoke for startup ordering). Will tag @Devon and @Priya.
Devon: Ping me on the PR; I'll review the Node and Rust bits.
Priya: I'll review the redaction tests. Also add a quick note in the onboarding README: "Call initLogger before importing instrumented modules; include a simple request-id fallback for first-logs; prefer SDK >=1.4.1."
Casey: Thanks all 44d
